The HashMap Class,A Simple Cache,"Use a HashMap to simulate a simple cache. Before doing a slow calculation, check if the result is already in the map."
The HashMap Class,A Simple Phone Book,"Create a HashMap to act as a phone book, mapping names (String) to phone numbers (String)."
The HashMap Class,Adding a Key-Value Pair (`put`),"Create a HashMap and use the `put()` method to add three entries: (101, 'Alice'), (102, 'Bob'), and (103, 'Charlie')."
The HashMap Class,Adding to a List in a HashMap,"Write the code to add a new item to the list of an existing category in your HashMap of lists."
The HashMap Class,Cache Logic (Cache Hit),"Write the code for a 'cache hit' - where the result is found in the map and returned immediately."
The HashMap Class,Cache Logic (Cache Miss),"Write the code for a 'cache miss' - where the result is not in the map, so you must calculate it and then `put()` it in the map for next time."
The HashMap Class,Character Frequency Counter,"Write a program that counts the frequency of each character in a string using a `HashMap<Character, Integer>`."
The HashMap Class,Checking a Permission,"Write a function that takes a permission name (String) and returns the boolean value from your permissions map."
The HashMap Class,Checking if Empty (`isEmpty`),"Create a new, empty HashMap and use the `isEmpty()` method to print whether it is empty. Then add an element and check again."
The HashMap Class,Checking if a Key Exists (`containsKey`),"Write an `if` statement that uses `containsKey()` to check if the key 103 is in your HashMap and prints a confirmation message."
The HashMap Class,Checking if a Value Exists (`containsValue`),"Write code to check if your HashMap contains the value 'Bob' and print the boolean result."
The HashMap Class,Clearing the HashMap (`clear`),"Add several elements to a HashMap, print its size, then use the `clear()` method to remove all entries. Print the size again."
The HashMap Class,Cloning a HashMap,"Create a HashMap, add data to it, and then create a shallow copy of it using the `clone()` method or the copy constructor."
The HashMap Class,Concept of Key-Value Pairs,"In a comment, explain in your own words what a key-value pair is. Provide a real-world example, like a dictionary."
The HashMap Class,Conditional Replace,"Use the overloaded `replace()` method that takes three arguments to replace a value only if the key is currently mapped to a specific old value."
The HashMap Class,Creating a HashMap,"Write the code to import `java.util.HashMap` and declare a new, empty HashMap that stores student IDs (Integer) as keys and student names (String) as values."
The HashMap Class,Creating a New List in a HashMap,"Write the logic to handle adding an item for a brand new category (i.e., you have to create the list first)."
The HashMap Class,Creating a Word Counter,"Create a HashMap to count word frequencies. The keys will be words (String) and values will be their counts (Integer)."
The HashMap Class,Different Data Types,"Create a HashMap that maps product names (String) to their prices (Double)."
The HashMap Class,Final Project - Configuration,"Use a `HashMap<String, String>` to store application settings (e.g. 'username' -> 'admin', 'theme' -> 'dark'). Write code to load and retrieve a setting."
The HashMap Class,Final Project - Gradebook,"Create a gradebook using a `HashMap<String, Integer>`. Write a small program that allows a user to add a student, remove a student, and view a student's grade."
The HashMap Class,Finding the Most Frequent Character,"After building your character frequency map, write code to loop through it and find the character with the highest count."
The HashMap Class,Getting a Value That Doesn't Exist,"Try to `get()` a value for a key that is not in the HashMap (e.g. 999). Print the result to see what `get()` returns."
The HashMap Class,Getting the Size (`size`),"After adding several entries to your HashMap, use the `size()` method to print the total number of key-value pairs it contains."
The HashMap Class,HashMap of Lists,"Create a HashMap where the key is a category (String) and the value is a `List<String>` of items in that category (e.g. 'Fruits' -> ['Apple', 'Banana'])."
The HashMap Class,Inventory Management,"Create a `HashMap<String, Integer>` to represent an inventory of products, mapping product names to their stock quantity."
The HashMap Class,Iterating Over Entries,"Use a `for-each` loop with `entrySet()` to iterate through the HashMap and print each entry in the format 'Key: [key], Value: [value]'."
The HashMap Class,Iterating Over Keys,"Use a `for-each` loop with `keySet()` to iterate through and print each key in your HashMap on a new line."
The HashMap Class,Iterating Over Values,"Use a `for-each` loop with `values()` to iterate through and print each value in your HashMap."
The HashMap Class,Looking Up a Phone Number,"Write code to look up and print a person's phone number from your phone book map."
The HashMap Class,Merging Two HashMaps,"Create two HashMaps and use the `putAll()` method to merge all the entries from the second map into the first."
The HashMap Class,Null Keys,"Demonstrate that a HashMap can have one (and only one) `null` key."
The HashMap Class,Null Values,"Demonstrate that a HashMap can store a `null` value by `put()`-ing an entry where the value is `null`."
The HashMap Class,Order of Elements,"Add several elements to a HashMap and print it multiple times. In a comment, note that the order is not guaranteed to be the same as the insertion order."
The HashMap Class,Permissions Check,"Use a `HashMap<String, Boolean>` to store user permissions (e.g. 'canEdit' -> true, 'canDelete' -> false)."
The HashMap Class,Removing a Key-Value Pair (`remove`),"Use the `remove()` method to delete the entry with the key 102 from your HashMap. Verify by checking its size or the key."
The HashMap Class,Replacing a Value (`replace`),"Use the `replace()` method to change the value for a key only if that key already exists in the map."
The HashMap Class,Retrieving a User Object,"Retrieve a `User` object from your map and call a method on it (e.g. `user.getName()`)."
The HashMap Class,Retrieving a Value (`get`),"From the HashMap you created, use the `get()` method to retrieve and print the name associated with the key 102."
The HashMap Class,Storing User Objects,"Create a simple `User` class with an ID and name. Create a HashMap that maps the user's ID (Integer) to the `User` object itself."
The HashMap Class,The `getOrDefault` Method,"Use the `getOrDefault()` method to get the value for an existing key. Then, use it to get a value for a non-existent key, providing a default value like 'Not Found'."
The HashMap Class,The `putIfAbsent` Method,"Use `putIfAbsent()` to try and add an entry with a key that already exists. Then, use it to add an entry with a new key. Observe the difference."
The HashMap Class,Understanding Generics,"In your HashMap declaration `HashMap<Integer, String>`, identify what the `<Integer>` and `<String>` parts represent."
The HashMap Class,Updating Inventory,"Write code to decrease the stock of a product in your inventory map after a sale."
The HashMap Class,Updating a Phone Number,"Update an existing person's phone number in your phone book map."
The HashMap Class,Using `entrySet`,"Use the `entrySet()` method to get a Set of all the key-value pairs (entries). Print the entire set."
The HashMap Class,Using `keySet` to Get All Keys,"Use the `keySet()` method to get a Set of all the keys in your HashMap. Print the entire set."
The HashMap Class,Using `values` to Get All Values,"Use the `values()` method to get a Collection of all the values in your HashMap. Print the collection."
The HashMap Class,What Happens with Duplicate Keys,"Add a new entry to your HashMap with a key that already exists (e.g. 101) but a new value (e.g. 'Alicia'). Print the value for key 101 to see what happens."
The HashMap Class,Word Counter Logic (First Word),"For your word counter, write the logic to handle the first time a word is seen (i.e., it's not yet a key in the map)."
The HashMap Class,Word Counter Logic (Subsequent Words),"For your word counter, write the logic to handle a word that has already been seen (i.e., it's already a key, so you need to increment its value)."
